<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael Faraday</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1791–1867</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael Faraday was an English scientist who discovered electromagnetic induction in 1831, the principle behind electric generators and transformers. Largely self-educated, he worked at the Royal Institution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where he also made major discoveries in electrochemistry, built an early electric motor and gave popular lectures on science.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-faraday.jpg" alt="Michael Faraday" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michael_Faraday_sitting_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
